urity-and-privacy.md b/docs/security-and-privacy.md\n774\tindex 284b37019..74cdc393b 100644\n775\t--- a/docs/security-and-privacy.md\n776\t+++ b/docs/security-and-privacy.md\n777\t@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@ If your repository is **public**, this data is visible to the entire internet.\n778\t \n779\t ### What Entire redacts automatically\n780\t \n781\t-Entire automatically scans transcript and metadata content before writing it to the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Five always-on secret detection methods run during condensation, plus a conditional sixth pass for user-defined secret rules (see [Customizing redaction](#customizing-redaction) below) and an opt-in seventh pass for PII (see [Optional PII redaction](#optional-pii-redaction) below):\n782\t+Entire automatically scans transcript and metadata content before writing it to the `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch. Five always-on secret detection methods run during condensation, plus a conditional sixth pass for user-defined secret rules (see [Customizing redaction](#customizing-redaction) below), an opt-in seventh pass for PII (see [Optional PII redaction](#optional-pii-redaction) below), and an opt-in eighth pass that shells out to the OpenAI Privacy Filter model (see [Optional OpenAI Privacy Filter](#optional-openai-privacy-filter-opf) below):\n783\t \n784\t 1. **Entropy scoring** — Identifies high-entropy strings (Shannon entropy > 4.5) that look like randomly generated secrets, even if they don't match a known pattern.\n785\t 2. **Pattern matching** — Uses [Betterleaks](https://github.com/betterleaks/betterleaks) built-in rules to detect known secret formats.\n786\t@@ -60,6 +60,91 @@ Teams can add their own regex patterns via `custom_patterns`. Each key is a labe\n787\t \n788\t If a custom pattern itself reveals sensitive structure (e.g. an internal ID format), put it in `.entire/settings.local.json` (gitignored) instead of `.entire/settings.json`.\n789\t \n790\t+### Optional OpenAI Privacy Filter (`opf`)\n791\t+\n792\t+A separate, **opt-in** layer that shells out to the [OpenAI Privacy Filter](https://github.com/openai/privacy-filter) (`opf`) — a 1.5B-parameter token-classification model that finds names, emails, phone numbers, addresses, dates, URLs, account numbers, and secrets that pure regex can miss. Disabled by default. Runs *in addition to* the seven built-in layers, only at the condensation and export boundaries (never per-turn), so the agent loop's hot path stays on the fast pipeline.\n793\t+\n794\t+Prerequisites:\n795\t+\n796\t+```bash\n797\t+pip install opf\n798\t+```\n799\t+\n800\t+Verify `opf --help` works; the CLI defaults to resolving the binary via `$PATH`. Override with the `command` setting if you need a specific path.\n801\t+\n802\t+Enable in `.entire/settings.json`:\n803\t+\n804\t+```json\n805\t+{\n806\t+  \"redaction\": {\n807\t+    \"openai_privacy_filter\": {\n808\t+      \"enabled\": true,\n809\t+      \"categories\": {\n810\t+        \"private_person\": true\n811\t+      }\n812\t+    }\n813\t+  }\n814\t+}\n815\t+```\n816\t+\n817\t+Available categories (set to `true` to enable, `false` or omit to skip):\n818\t+\n819\t+| Category | Replacement token | Notes |\n820\t+|---|---|---|\n821\t+| `private_person` | `[REDACTED_PERSON]` | Person names |\n822\t+| `private_email` | `[REDACTED_EMAIL]` | Email addresses |\n823\t+| `private_phone` | `[REDACTED_PHONE]` | Phone numbers |\n824\t+| `private_address` | `[REDACTED_ADDRESS]` | Street addresses |\n825\t+| `private_url` | `[REDACTED_URL]` | URLs that may identify a person/account |\n826\t+| `private_date` | `[REDACTED_DATE]` | Dates (DOB, etc.) |\n827\t+| `account_number` | `[REDACTED_ACCOUNT_NUMBER]` | Account / card / SSN-shaped numbers |\n828\t+| `secret` | `REDACTED` | Generic credential-shaped values |\n829\t+\n830\t+Unknown category names are rejected at settings load time so typos surface immediately instead of silently disabling a category.\n831\t+\n832\t+Full settings reference:\n833\t+\n834\t+```json\n835\t+{\n836\t+  \"redaction\": {\n837\t+    \"openai_privacy_filter\": {\n838\t+      \"enabled\": true,\n839\t+      \"categories\": {\n840\t+        \"private_person\": true,\n841\t+        \"private_email\": true,\n842\t+        \"private_phone\": true,\n843\t+        \"private_address\": false,\n844\t+        \"private_url\": false,\n845\t+        \"private_date\": false,\n846\t+        \"account_number\": false,\n847\t+        \"secret\": false\n848\t+      },\n849\t+      \"command\": \"opf\",\n850\t+      \"timeout_seconds\": 30\n851\t+    }\n852\t+  }\n853\t+}\n854\t+```\n855\t+\n856\t+- `command` — path or PATH-resolvable name of the `opf` binary. Defaults to `opf`.\n857\t+- `timeout_seconds` — per-invocation timeout. Defaults to `30`.\n858\t+\n859\t+There is no `on_failure` setting; warn-on-failure is the only mode supported today. If OPF is not on PATH, fails to start, or times out, Entire prints a one-line `× OpenAI Privacy Filter unavailable …` notice and continues with the seven built-in layers. A per-process circuit breaker disables OPF for the remainder of the invocation after the first failure, so a broken install costs one warning rather than one timeout per redaction call.\n860\t+\n861\t+Cost note: each shell-out loads the OPF model (~1.5B parameters on CPU). Condensation batches all eligible leaf strings into a single inference pass per scope (transcript + joined prompts), so a typical real-world commit adds ~25–30s of OPF inference rather than the multi-minute cost a per-leaf flow would incur.\n862\t+\n863\t+Verifying it's working:\n864\t+\n865\t+```fish\n866\t+# After enabling OPF, run an agent turn that includes a name in the prompt,\n867\t+# e.g. \"Create notes.txt with: Alice Johnson reviewed the proposal.\"\n868\t+# Then commit and inspect the latest checkpoint:\n869\t+git log --oneline entire/checkpoints/v1 | head -2\n870\t+entire checkpoint explain HEAD | grep -i 'REDACTED_PERSON'\n871\t+```\n872\t+\n873\t+If `[REDACTED_PERSON]` appears in the prompt or transcript section, OPF is active.\n874\t+\n875\t ### Recommendations\n876\t \n877\t If your AI sessions will touch sensitive data:\n878\tdiff --git a/redact/opf.go b/redact/opf.go\n879\tnew file mode 100644\n880\tindex 000000000..c1a4db09d\n881\t--- /dev/null\n882\t+++ b/redact/opf.go\n883\t@@ -0,0 +1,436 @@\n884\t+package redact\n885\t+\n886\t+import (\n887\t+\t\"bytes\"\n888\t+\t\"context\"\n889\t+\t\"encoding/json\"\n890\t+\t\"errors\"\n891\t+\t\"fmt\"\n892\t+\t\"io\"\n893\t+\t\"log/slog\"\n894\t+\t\"os\"\n895\t+\t\"os/exec\"\n896\t+\t\"sort\"\n897\t+\t\"strings\"\n898\t+\t\"sync\"\n899\t+\t\"sync/atomic\"\n900\t+\t\"time\"\n901\t+\t\"unicode/utf8\"\n902\t+)\n903\t+\n904\t+// OPFConfig configures the optional OpenAI Privacy Filter detection layer.\n905\t+// Defaults are applied by ConfigurePrivacyFilter; callers should pass values\n906\t+// straight from settings without local normalization.\n907\t+type OPFConfig struct {\n908\t+\tEnabled    bool\n909\t+\tCategories map[string]bool\n910\t+\tCommand    string // path or name of the opf binary; \"\" defaults to \"opf\"\n911\t+\tTimeout    int    // seconds; 0 defaults to 30\n912\t+\n913\t+\t// runtime is private and constructed by ConfigurePrivacyFilter.\n914\t+\t// Tests inject a fake via ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime.\n915\t+\truntime opfRuntime\n916\t+}\n917\t+\n918\t+var (\n919\t+\topfConfig   *OPFConfig\n920\t+\topfConfigMu sync.RWMutex\n921\t+\n922\t+\t// opfBreakerTripped, once set, disables OPF for the rest of this process.\n923\t+\t// The first detectOPF failure trips it via handleOPFFailure; subsequent\n924\t+\t// detectOPF calls short-circuit before shelling out. Without this, a\n925\t+\t// broken OPF install (binary missing, persistently timing out, etc.)\n926\t+\t// makes every redaction call pay the full timeout — turning one\n927\t+\t// condensation into N × 30s waits instead of a single warning plus\n928\t+\t// graceful fallback. Process-scoped so a fresh CLI invocation re-attempts.\n929\t+\topfBreakerTripped atomic.Bool\n930\t+)\n931\t+\n932\t+// ConfigurePrivacyFilter sets the global OPF configuration and constructs\n933\t+// the default shell-out runtime. Call once at process startup after loading\n934\t+// settings. Thread-safe. Subsequent calls replace the previous configuration\n935\t+// and reset the circuit breaker (a new config might fix what broke the prior\n936\t+// one).\n937\t+func ConfigurePrivacyFilter(cfg OPFConfig) {\n938\t+\tcfgCopy := cfg\n939\t+\tif cfgCopy.Timeout <= 0 {\n940\t+\t\tcfgCopy.Timeout = 30\n941\t+\t}\n942\t+\tif cfgCopy.Command == \"\" {\n943\t+\t\tcfgCopy.Command = \"opf\"\n944\t+\t}\n945\t+\tcfgCopy.runtime = newShellOut(cfgCopy.Command, cfgCopy.Timeout)\n946\t+\topfConfigMu.Lock()\n947\t+\topfConfig = &cfgCopy\n948\t+\topfConfigMu.Unlock()\n949\t+\topfBreakerTripped.Store(false)\n950\t+}\n951\t+\n952\t+// ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime is the test-only variant that takes an\n953\t+// explicit runtime instead of constructing one.\n954\t+func ConfigurePrivacyFilterWithRuntime(cfg OPFConfig, rt opfRuntime) {\n955\t+\tcfgCopy := cfg\n956\t+\tif cfgCopy.Timeout <= 0 {\n957\t+\t\tcfgCopy.Timeout = 30\n958\t+\t}\n959\t+\tcfgCopy.runtime = rt\n960\t+\topfConfigMu.Lock()\n961\t+\topfConfig = &cfgCopy\n962\t+\topfConfigMu.Unlock()\n963\t+\topfBreakerTripped.Store(false)\n964\t+}\n965\t+\n966\t+func getOPFConfig() *OPFConfig {\n967\t+\topfConfigMu.RLock()\n968\t+\tdefer opfConfigMu.RUnlock()\n969\t+\treturn opfConfig\n970\t+}\n971\t+\n972\t+// GetOPFConfigForTest returns the current OPF configuration, or nil if\n973\t+// never configured. The \"ForTest\" suffix signals test-only intent — callers\n974\t+// must be limited to test files in other packages.\n975\t+func GetOPFConfigForTest() *OPFConfig {\n976\t+\treturn getOPFConfig()\n977\t+}\n978\t+\n979\t+// ResetOPFConfigForTest clears configuration and the circuit breaker. Used\n980\t+// by tests in other packages to return to a \"never configured\" state.\n981\t+func ResetOPFConfigForTest() {\n982\t+\tresetOPFConfig()\n983\t+}\n984\t+\n985\t+func resetOPFConfig() {\n986\t+\topfConfigMu.Lock()\n987\t+\topfConfig = nil\n988\t+\topfConfigMu.Unlock()\n989\t+\topfBreakerTripped.Store(false)\n990\t+}\n991\t+\n992\t+// opfLabelMap maps OPF native labels to our taggedRegion.label values.\n993\t+// Empty mapped value renders as the bare \"REDACTED\" token; non-empty values\n994\t+// render as \"[REDACTED_<LABEL>]\" via the replacementToken helper.\n995\t+var opfLabelMap = map[string]string{\n996\t+\t\"private_person\":  \"PERSON\",\n997\t+\t\"private_email\":   \"EMAIL\",\n998\t+\t\"private_phone\":   \"PHONE\",\n999\t+\t\"private_address\": \"ADDRESS\",\n1000\t+\t\"private_url\":     \"URL\",\n1001\t+\t\"private_date\":    \"DATE\",\n1002\t+\t\"account_number\":  \"ACCOUNT_NUMBER\",\n1003\t+\t\"secret\":          \"\", // -> bare REDACTED\n1004\t+}\n1005\t+\n1006\t+// IsKnownOPFCategory reports whether name is one of the OPF native labels\n1007\t+// the CLI knows how to tag and render. Exported so the settings layer can\n1008\t+// reject typos at parse time — silent zero-detection of a privacy category\n1009\t+// would leave users thinking they're protected when they're not.\n1010\t+func IsKnownOPFCategory(name string) bool {\n1011\t+\t_, ok := opfLabelMap[name]\n1012\t+\treturn ok\n1013\t+}\n1014\t+\n1015\t+func mapOPFLabel(opfLabel string) string {\n1016\t+\tif mapped, ok := opfLabelMap[opfLabel]; ok {\n1017\t+\t\treturn mapped\n1018\t+\t}\n1019\t+\treturn \"\"\n1020\t+}\n1021\t+\n1022\t+func enabledCategories(cfg *OPFConfig) []string {\n1023\t+\tif cfg == nil {\n1024\t+\t\treturn nil\n1025\t+\t}\n1026\t+\tout := make([]string, 0, len(cfg.Categories))\n1027\t+\tfor label, enabled := range cfg.Categories {\n1028\t+\t\tif !enabled {\n1029\t+\t\t\tcontinue\n1030\t+\t\t}\n1031\t+\t\tif _, known := opfLabelMap[label]; !known {\n1032\t+\t\t\tcontinue\n1033\t+\t\t}\n1034\t+\t\tout = append(out, label)\n1035\t+\t}\n1036\t+\tsort.Strings(out) // deterministic order for tests + logs\n1037\t+\treturn out\n1038\t+}\n1039\t+\n1040\t+// opfStderr is where progress and failure UX is written. Defaults to\n1041\t+// /dev/tty so messages survive the post-commit hook's stderr redirect\n1042\t+// (the hook runs `entire hooks git post-commit 2>/dev/null` to suppress\n1043\t+// unrelated logging). Tests override this directly.\n1044\t+var opfStderr = openTTYOrDiscard()\n1045\t+\n1046\t+func openTTYOrDiscard() io.Writer {\n1047\t+\tf, err := os.OpenFile(\"/dev/tty\", os.O_WRONLY, 0)\n1048\t+\tif err != nil {\n1049\t+\t\treturn io.Discard\n1050\t+\t}\n1051\t+\treturn f\n1052\t+}\n1053\t+\n1054\t+// detectOPF runs OPF on a single text and returns tagged regions for any\n1055\t+// spans whose category is enabled in cfg. Returns nil when OPF is disabled,\n1056\t+// unconfigured, the breaker is tripped, the input is too short to be prose,\n1057\t+// the input has no enabled categories, or the runtime returns an error.\n1058\t+//\n1059\t+// The has-space gate eliminates structural strings (paths, IDs, snake_case\n1060\t+// keys) that would otherwise pay the OPF cold-start with zero benefit.\n1061\t+// Single-token PII shapes (e.g. an isolated email) are caught by the regex\n1062\t+// layers.\n1063\t+func detectOPF(ctx context.Context, cfg *OPFConfig, s string) []taggedRegion {\n1064\t+\tif cfg == nil || !cfg.Enabled || cfg.runtime == nil || s == \"\" {\n1065\t+\t\treturn nil\n1066\t+\t}\n1067\t+\tif opfBreakerTripped.Load() {\n1068\t+\t\treturn nil\n1069\t+\t}\n1070\t+\tif !strings.ContainsRune(s, ' ') {\n1071\t+\t\treturn nil\n1072\t+\t}\n1073\t+\tcats := enabledCategories(cfg)\n1074\t+\tif len(cats) == 0 {\n1075\t+\t\treturn nil\n1076\t+\t}\n1077\t+\n1078\t+\tfmt.Fprintln(opfStderr, \"→ OpenAI Privacy Filter: scanning transcript…\")\n1079\t+\tstart := time.Now()\n1080\t+\tspans, err := cfg.runtime.Redact(ctx, s, cats)\n1081\t+\tif err != nil {\n1082\t+\t\thandleOPFFailure(ctx, cfg, err)\n1083\t+\t\treturn nil\n1084\t+\t}\n1085\t+\tfmt.Fprintf(opfStderr, \"✓ OpenAI Privacy Filter: done (%.1fs)\\n\", time.Since(start).Seconds())\n1086\t+\n1087\t+\tout := make([]taggedRegion, 0, len(spans))\n1088\t+\tfor _, sp := range spans {\n1089\t+\t\tif !cfg.Categories[sp.Label] {\n1090\t+\t\t\tcontinue\n1091\t+\t\t}\n1092\t+\t\tif sp.Start < 0 || sp.End > len(s) || sp.Start >= sp.End {\n1093\t+\t\t\tcontinue\n1094\t+\t\t}\n1095\t+\t\tout = append(out, taggedRegion{\n1096\t+\t\t\tregion: region{sp.Start, sp.End},\n1097\t+\t\t\tlabel:  mapOPFLabel(sp.Label),\n1098\t+\t\t})\n1099\t+\t}\n1100\t+\treturn out\n1101\t+}\n1102\t+\n1103\t+// handleOPFFailure trips the circuit breaker and emits one user-facing\n1104\t+// warning. CompareAndSwap ensures only the FIRST failure produces output;\n1105\t+// subsequent detectOPF calls short-circuit before reaching here, so the\n1106\t+// swap is mostly a guard against concurrent first-call races.\n1107\t+func handleOPFFailure(ctx context.Context, cfg *OPFConfig, err error) {\n1108\t+\tif !opfBreakerTripped.CompareAndSwap(false, true) {\n1109\t+\t\treturn\n1110\t+\t}\n1111\t+\tslog.WarnContext(ctx, \"OpenAI Privacy Filter call failed; disabling for the rest of this process\",\n1112\t+\t\tslog.String(\"component\", \"redaction\"),\n1113\t+\t\tslog.String(\"command\", cfg.Command),\n1114\t+\t\tslog.String(\"error\", err.Error()),\n1115\t+\t)\n1116\t+\tfmt.Fprintf(opfStderr, \"× OpenAI Privacy Filter unavailable (%s); falling back to regex layers for the rest of this commit. Install with 'pip install opf'.\\n\", cfg.Command)\n1117\t+}\n1118\t+\n1119\t+// Span is a redaction region returned by an opfRuntime, with BYTE-offset\n1120\t+// boundaries against the input text. OPF itself reports character (rune)\n1121\t+// offsets via its JSON output; the shell-out adapter translates those to\n1122\t+// byte offsets before returning Spans so callers can slice []byte input\n1123\t+// directly without re-walking runes.\n1124\t+type Span struct {\n1125\t+\tStart int\n1126\t+\tEnd   int\n1127\t+\tLabel string // OPF native label, e.g. \"private_person\"\n1128\t+}\n1129\t+\n1130\t+// opfRuntime is the abstraction over the OpenAI Privacy Filter binary or\n1131\t+// (future) daemon. Implementations must be safe for concurrent use.\n1132\t+type opfRuntime interface {\n1133\t+\tRedact(ctx context.Context, text string, categories []string) ([]Span, error)\n1134\t+\tRedactBatch(ctx context.Context, inputs []string, categories []string) ([][]Span, error)\n1135\t+}\n1136\t+\n1137\t+// shellOut runs the user-installed `opf` binary per call. Cold-start every\n1138\t+// invocation is intentional for v1 — daemon mode is a planned follow-up\n1139\t+// that implements the same opfRuntime interface so callers don't change.\n1140\t+type shellOut struct {\n1141\t+\tcommand        string\n1142\t+\ttimeoutSeconds int\n1143\t+\n1144\t+\t// commandRunner builds the *exec.Cmd run for each Redact call. Tests\n1145\t+\t// override this with a closure returning a Cmd wrapping a shell snippet.\n1146\t+\tcommandRunner func(ctx context.Context, name string, args ...string) *exec.Cmd\n1147\t+}\n1148\t+\n1149\t+func newShellOut(command string, timeoutSeconds int) *shellOut {\n1150\t+\treturn &shellOut{\n1151\t+\t\tcommand:        command,\n1152\t+\t\ttimeoutSeconds: timeoutSeconds,\n1153\t+\t\tcommandRunner:  exec.CommandContext,\n1154\t+\t}\n1155\t+}\n1156\t+\n1157\t+// opfBatchSeparator joins inputs into a single opf invocation. opf treats\n1158\t+// '\\n' as a per-input delimiter and runs a fresh inference pass per line,\n1159\t+// which is no faster than per-call shell-out. Joining with a non-newline\n1160\t+// separator instead causes opf to treat the concatenation as ONE input and\n1161\t+// do ONE inference pass, amortizing the model load across all inputs.\n1162\t+//\n1163\t+// ASCII RECORD SEPARATOR (U+001E) satisfies both requirements:\n1164\t+//  1. Doesn't appear in real text (so no collision with content)\n1165\t+//  2. Looks like whitespace to opf's tokenizer (so it doesn't confuse\n1166\t+//     span boundaries)\n1167\t+const opfBatchSeparator = \"\\x1e\"\n1168\t+\n1169\t+// Redact runs OPF on a single text input.\n1170\t+func (s *shellOut) Redact(ctx context.Context, text string, categories []string) ([]Span, error) {\n1171\t+\tif len(categories) == 0 {\n1172\t+\t\treturn nil, nil\n1173\t+\t}\n1174\t+\tbatch, err := s.RedactBatch(ctx, []string{text}, categories)\n1175\t+\tif err != nil || len(batch) == 0 {\n1176\t+\t\treturn nil, err\n1177\t+\t}\n1178\t+\treturn batch[0], nil\n1179\t+}\n1180\t+\n1181\t+// RedactBatch sends multiple inputs to opf as a single shell-out, joined\n1182\t+// with opfBatchSeparator. Internal newlines in inputs are flattened to\n1183\t+// spaces (1-byte → 1-byte, offsets stay valid). opf emits one JSON object\n1184\t+// covering the whole concatenated text; spans are partitioned back per\n1185\t+// input via partitionIndex. Spans crossing a separator boundary are dropped.\n1186\t+//\n1187\t+// Errors deliberately do NOT include stdout or stderr content — OPF can\n1188\t+// echo input fragments to either stream when misconfigured, and the error\n1189\t+// is later logged + printed to TTY by handleOPFFailure. Byte counts are\n1190\t+// sufficient for diagnostics.\n1191\t+func (s *shellOut) RedactBatch(ctx context.Context, inputs []string, categories []string) ([][]Span, error) {\n1192\t+\tif len(inputs) == 0 || len(categories) == 0 {\n1193\t+\t\treturn nil, nil\n1194\t+\t}\n1195\t+\ttimeout := time.Duration(s.timeoutSeconds) * time.Second\n1196\t+\tcallCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, timeout)\n1197\t+\tdefer cancel()\n1198\t+\n1199\t+\tvar buf strings.Builder\n1200\t+\tstarts := make([]int, len(inputs))\n1201\t+\tfor i, in := range inputs {\n1202\t+\t\tif i > 0 {\n1203\t+\t\t\tbuf.WriteString(opfBatchSeparator)\n1204\t+\t\t}\n1205\t+\t\tstarts[i] = buf.Len()\n1206\t+\t\tbuf.WriteString(strings.ReplaceAll(in, \"\\n\", \" \"))\n1207\t+\t}\n1208\t+\tbatched := buf.String()\n1209\t