{"parentUuid":"993c18b0-5ab9-4249-aec5-00a6fe345afd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01UpBEigyVvVL6Up6hx6TiQ9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, new commits on `entire/checkpoints/v1` — and to your second question: I think it actually is the best fit, *given* the constraints of this codebase. Let me explain why before defaulting to it.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- `entire/checkpoints/v1` is CLI-owned infrastructure, not a user-facing branch. Nothing references its commit SHAs externally — the `Entire-Checkpoint: <12-hex>` trailer on the user's main-branch commits links by **tree path** (`<id[:2]>/<id[2:]>/`), not by commit SHA. So rewriting those commits is invisible to every other part of the system, including the user's own commit history.\n- Git's content-addressing model means there's no real distinction between \"amending\" and \"creating a new commit\" — any byte change produces a new SHA. The only question is whether you keep the old commit reachable somewhere (we shouldn't — it's a transient 7-layer-only stepping stone).\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n**Why \"rewrite at pre-push\" wins over the alternatives:**\n\n| Approach | What goes on remote | Local debuggability | Architectural cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Rewrite commits at pre-push** (what I proposed) | 8-layer | Brief: 7-layer visible between commit and push | Low — pre-push hook already exists |\n| Two parallel branches (`v1` local, `v1-redacted` remote) | 8-layer | Permanent: 7-layer stays local | Medium — doubles cleanup, status, explain logic |\n| Side-car metadata (store OPF spans, apply at render time) | 7-layer bytes + spans JSON | Trivial | **Security regression** — raw bytes on remote aren't actually redacted |\n| Async background OPF after commit | 8-layer eventually | Complex (in-flight state) | High — background processes, crash recovery, coordination |\n\nSide-car (the third row) is the worst — it defeats the point of OPF as a redaction layer because anyone running `git cat-file blob` on the remote would see the unredacted-by-OPF text. The \"redaction\" only exists in our renderer, not in the bytes. Hard pass.\n\n**The one design decision worth being deliberate about**: do we keep the 7-layer-only commits reachable somewhere after the rewrite, or do we let them become unreachable and GC'd?\n\n- **GC'd (simpler, recommended)**: branch ref moves to the new 8-layer tip. Old commits become unreachable and disappear on the next `git gc`. No audit trail of \"what OPF added.\"\n- **Preserved as a debug ref** (e.g. `entire/checkpoints/v1-pre-opf/<sha>`): adds a refs namespace, useful for \"show me what OPF caught\" forensics, but adds cleanup logic and storage.\n\nI'd go GC'd for v1. If users ever ask for \"what did OPF actually redact in my last commit?\" — that's a feature we can add later by keeping the old SHA briefly.\n\n**One subtlety to handle correctly**: if a user pushes multiple times, each push only needs to rewrite checkpoints that haven't been OPF-processed yet. We can track this with a trailer on each `entire/checkpoints/v1` commit (`Entire-OPF-Applied: true`), so the second push doesn't re-redact what's already 8-layer.\n\nSound good? If yes, I can write up the implementation plan with concrete file boundaries."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":261869,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":4970,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":261869,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4970,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":261869,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":261869},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standar