{"parentUuid":"d593ab3a-6ae0-48bd-af96-454add8b2081","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01GbtsfGJJ1qksy8PocxQRyj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good catch — yes, briefly, locally, in three places. Worth understanding the actual mechanics:\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- Git is **content-addressable and append-only at the object layer**. Moving a branch ref doesn't *delete* the old commit — it just makes it unreachable. The object stays in `.git/objects/` until `git gc --prune` sweeps it, which by default only runs after `gc.pruneExpire` (2 weeks).\n- **Worktrees share `.git/objects/`** — so the old object is visible from any of your 73 worktrees, not just the one that did the rewrite.\n- The reflog is the second hidden persistence layer: even if you never run `git cat-file -p OLD_SHA`, `git reflog show entire/checkpoints/v1` records every ref move with timestamps, keeping a paper trail for ~90 days (`gc.reflogExpire`).\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n**Where the old 7-layer-only objects live after the rewrite:**\n\n| Location | Persistence | Pushed to remote? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.git/objects/` (unreachable loose/packed objects) | Until next `git gc --prune` (default: 2 weeks) | No — `git push` only sends reachable objects |\n| `git reflog entire/checkpoints/v1` | Default 90 days, then expired | No — reflog is strictly local |\n| Pack files (if already packed) | Same as loose objects, but `git gc` only repacks based on unreachable thresholds | No |\n\nSo nothing reaches the remote, but a local user can recover the 7-layer version for ~2 weeks via:\n```\ngit cat-file -p $(git reflog show entire/checkpoints/v1 | awk 'NR==2 {print $1}')\n```\n\n**Does this matter?** Depends on your threat model:\n\n- **\"Don't leak PII to remote / shared infra\"** — fine. The remote never sees it. This is the typical OPF use case.\n- **\"Don't leave PII recoverable anywhere, even locally\"** — needs explicit cleanup. After the rewrite, run:\n  ```\n  git reflog expire --expire-unreachable=now refs/entire/checkpoints/v1\n  git gc --prune=now\n  ```\n  …but this is I/O-heavy and slow on big repos. Doing it on every push would tax users.\n\n**My recommendation**: don't aggressively prune by default. The user already has the raw transcripts in `.entire/` session files (and on the shadow branch `entire/<commit>-<worktree>`), so the OPF-pre-redaction version isn't disclosing anything new to anyone with local access. **Document the persistence window** in `docs/security-and-privacy.md` so users with stricter threat models can opt into the heavier cleanup.\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- The honest framing here: OPF protects against **what leaves the machine**, not against forensic recovery on the machine itself. The raw transcripts under `.entire/` are themselves unredacted — they're the source from which all redacted views are derived. Pretending OPF guarantees local-machine privacy would be a misrepresentation of what the layer actually does.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\nWant me to draft the implementation plan now (post-commit reverts to 7-layer, pre-push does OPF rewrite + push, with the prompt UX layered on top), or would you rather we explore any other angle first?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4992,"cache_read_input_tokens":261869,"output_tokens":3262,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4992,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":3262,"cache_read_input_tokens":261869,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4992,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4992},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagn